Relate protected operations to a serial specification and test broken designs (@computing-serial-histories: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Separate-safety-from-progress-and-account-for-waiting.lingua#r_00BBBFDC1DF17364A8D5372BB8") { r_00E7F13A69868429E44F8AC107
Specify the abstract counter as one atomic operation add(a), whose effect is x←x+a. In the concrete locked protocol, acquisition attempts, local arithmetic and release are additional internal steps. Choose the protected write as the operation's abstract effect point. At that point the invariant proves that x changes by precisely a from the sum of earlier committed jobs. Writes are totally ordered, and mutual exclusion ensures each uses the result of its predecessor. Removing the internal steps therefore yields a valid serial sequence of abstract additions.

If an operation is invoked before its first acquire attempt and responds after release, that effect point lies within its invocation/response interval. Nonoverlapping operations retain their real-time order. This is the beginning of a linearizability argument under the declared model. The program exposes no per-operation returned value, no separate read operation and no multi-field object. Those would need their own history specification and proof. Merely observing the correct final sum would not prove such a richer history contract; addition can conceal order distinctions.

Two deliberate protocol changes clarify the actual mechanism. In the split variant, replace acquire by check then set. Both actors can check zero before either sets one. They can then both enter and read the old value. At x₀=0 with unit increments, this legal sequence ends at one: check₀, check₁, set₀, read₀, add₀, set₁, read₁, write₀, release₀, add₁, write₁, release₁. Clearing the flag while another actor is inside further breaks its intended meaning. Individual reads and writes being atomic did not make the compound check-and-set atomic.

In the early-release variant, keep atomic acquire but release after the local addition and before the shared write. A legal sequence is acquire₀, read₀, add₀, release₀, acquire₁, read₁, write₀, add₁, release₁, write₁. Again the final value is one. Only one actor need hold the flag at a time, yet its protection does not cover the complete update invariant. A lock around an arbitrary convenient section of code is insufficient.

The original graph explorer retains parents for first-reached states and reconstructs a shortest transition path to a bad terminal state. Replaying that path checks that the witness is legal in the model. For two unit increments, the split variant has 72 reachable states and early release has 34; both include terminal counters one and two. These finite totals are properties of the declared representation, including reset local state. They are not measures of a real implementation's failure rate or performance.

**Read and work:** [Operating Systems Three Easy Pieces](../sources/Operating-Systems-Three-Easy-Pieces.lingua#r_009CB72626232ED2E08E80BFC4), chapter 28's failed load/store lock and atomic primitive discussion. Annotate both counterexample histories with x, flag and each local value. State the first point where the locked invariant fails. Propose a different two-field specification, such as total count plus a matching checksum, and identify why protecting each field separately might fail the combined invariant. Treat that as a new analysis, not as an already checked program result. Retain the earlier [Distinguish write acceptance atomicity ordering and persistence](Storage-contracts-and-persistence-order.lingua#r_00C17999D9A870BEF97CB7A385) distinction: atomicity of visibility and durability after failure answer different questions.

**Continue:** [Separate safety from progress and account for waiting](Separate-safety-from-progress-and-account-for-waiting.lingua#r_00BBBFDC1DF17364A8D5372BB8).

